await write('overview', [head + ' — Big-picture orientation and mental model', '', '# Problem and purpose', '', 'Operational readiness asks two different questions. Can the service continue when a component fails? And can we recover the correct data after loss, corruption, or a bad deployment? High availability, disaster recovery, backups, schema migrations, and row history each answer only part of that problem.', '', '# Plain-language mental model', '', 'Think of backups as durable checkpoints plus a sequence of log records that lets you replay the database to a chosen point. A restore is a specific recipe: choose the right checkpoint, apply compatible changes in order, then open the database at the intended recovery point. Replicas keep another copy ready but can copy mistakes too. Migrations change the shape of data while old and new application versions may still be running. Temporal tables retain row versions for queries, with a retention policy, but they are not backups.', '', '# How the parts fit together', '', '1. Set RPO (acceptable data loss) and RTO (acceptable time to restore service) for each workload.', '2. Choose recovery model, full/differential/log schedule, secure retention, and encryption-key custody to meet the data-recovery target.', '3. Choose HA/DR topology and test failover eligibility, quorum, lag, client routing, and application reconnection.', '4. Deploy schema changes in compatible steps; make data backfills restartable and observable.', '5. Run restore, failover, integrity, and migration rehearsals that measure actual outcomes.', '', '# Concept map', '', '| Mechanism | Main question it answers | Important limit |', '| --- | --- | --- |', '| Backup plus restore chain | Can data be recovered to a tested point? | Requires the correct media, keys, sequence, and time |', '| Availability replica | Can service resume on another copy? | Logical mistakes can replicate; asynchronous replicas can lag |', '| Replication/CDC/change tracking | How can changes reach another consumer? | Not equivalent to a backup or zero-loss HA |', '| Temporal table | What row version was valid at a database time? | Retention and system time do not establish actor/intent |', '| Expand-and-contract deployment | Can app versions coexist during a schema change? | Destructive cleanup must wait until old code is gone |', '', '# End-to-end walkthrough', '', 'Assume an operator deletes customer rows at 10:02 and the recovery objective permits at most five minutes of data loss. A tested full backup, later differential, and frequent log backups may allow a point-in-time restore to an isolated database just before 10:02, provided the chain and keys are intact and the recovery model supports the target. Validate consistency and application data, then decide how to reconcile valid writes after the restore point before cutover. An availability-group secondary may already contain the deletion, so simply failing over does not undo it. A temporal history query might recover prior row values if retention and versioning were enabled, but it does not restore the whole database or its relationships.', '', '# When to use it and tradeoffs', '', 'Use synchronous replicas for low-loss failover where latency and topology permit; use asynchronous replicas when distance/latency matters and explicitly accept lag risk. Use backups for independent recoverability and retention. Replication/CDC/change tracking are data movement or synchronization mechanisms with distinct payloads. Temporal tables help reconstruct row history; ledger addresses tamper-evidence. All require key, access, capacity, and retention planning.', '', '# What later lessons cover', '', 'The first reference lesson covers backup/restore chains and recovery objectives. The second distinguishes availability and change movement. The third covers schema evolution, temporal history, integrity checks, and rehearsal. Earlier topic t03-012 covers security fundamentals and t03-010 covers transaction behavior.', '', '# Recent version changes', '', 'SQL Server 2025 introduces ZSTD as a compressed-backup algorithm; compare CPU, backup and restore throughput, and storage before adopting it. [Microsoft backup compression documentation](' + urls.compression + ') Change event streaming remains marked Preview in the current SQL Server 2025 release-status material, even though the status table’s update column says RTM. Verify its support status before production use. [Release status](' + urls.changeEvents + ')', '', '# Sources', '', bibliography].join('\n'));

await write('concepts', [head + ' — Core concepts and how they fit together', '', '# Learning objectives', '', '- Design a restore path that meets stated RPO/RTO and prove it with an isolated restore.', '- Explain why recovery model, log continuity, backup types, and recovery options determine a valid restore sequence.', '- Distinguish replica failover, backup restore, replication, CDC, Change Tracking, and temporal history.', '- Plan a reversible migration across mixed application versions.', '', '# Prerequisites', '', 'Know database files, transaction logs, transactions, deployments, and basic availability concepts. Security topic t03-012 covers permissions and encryption fundamentals; this lesson focuses on operational key custody and recovery.', '', '# Core concepts', '', '## Define recovery outcomes before choosing a schedule', '', 'RPO is the maximum acceptable data-loss interval; RTO is the maximum acceptable service-restoration time. A frequent backup may support RPO but still miss RTO if restore is slow or untested. A replica may reduce failover time but can share logical errors and may lag. Include detection, decisions, restore/redo, integrity/application validation, client routing, and recovery of related services in the time budget.', '', '## Recovery model and chain determine where recovery can stop', '', 'SIMPLE supports data-backup recovery but no log backups or point-in-time recovery. FULL supports point-in-time recovery when log backups keep an unbroken chain. BULK_LOGGED changes how some bulk work is logged; a log backup containing such operations can prevent stopping inside that backup. Switching FULL to SIMPLE breaks the log chain; after switching back, take a data backup to start a new chain. A full backup does not replace routine log backups.', '', 'A conventional full backup establishes the differential base. A copy-only full does not change that base. Differential restore uses its base full plus the selected differential; log backups are applied after the data restore in correct LSN/fork order. Preserve backup headers/catalog metadata, filenames, checksums, keys, and tested scripts; never infer order from timestamps alone. [Recovery models](' + urls.recovery + ') [restore overview](' + urls.restoreOverview + ') [complete restore sequence](' + urls.completeRestore + ')', '', '## Restore is a stateful sequence', '', 'For multi-step recovery, restore the full and optional differential with `NORECOVERY`, then apply each required log in sequence, and finish with `RECOVERY` only at the target. `STOPAT` must identify a time covered by the log chain; use the same target on log restore steps. In BULK_LOGGED, a log backup containing minimally logged operations cannot be stopped at a time inside that backup. `RESTORE VERIFYONLY` reads/checks the set but does not prove restored database consistency. A restore rehearsal does. [Point-in-time restore](' + urls.pointRestore + ') [RESTORE options](' + urls.restoreArgs + ')', '', '## Keep recovery media and keys independent', '', 'Backups should survive loss or compromise of the primary data environment. Encryption protects backup media only if the required certificate/private key or asymmetric key remains separately available and tested. Retention must preserve all dependencies (full/differential/log) for each recovery target. Checksum, compression, and encryption each answer different concerns; they do not turn a backup into a validated recovery.', '', '## Availability and data movement are separate mechanisms', '', 'A synchronous replica hardens log before acknowledging commit when synchronized, adding network/commit latency. Automatic and planned no-data-loss failover require a synchronized eligible target and cluster health/quorum. An asynchronous replica can lag; forced failover may lose transactions not hardened there. A listener and client retry configuration help reconnect but do not prove the new primary is healthy.', '', 'FCI moves an instance between nodes, often over shared storage; an availability group moves selected database roles across SQL instances. Log shipping periodically copies/restores log backups. Transactional, snapshot, and merge replication distribute data under different policies. CDC exposes row-level change records; Change Tracking exposes that rows changed so consumers can fetch current rows. Neither is a complete backup. [Availability modes](' + urls.modes + ') [failover conditions](' + urls.failover + ') [replication/CDC/CT](' + urls.replication + ')', '', '## Schema change and history need their own lifecycle', '', 'Expand-and-contract creates a compatibility window: add first, deploy code that handles both shapes, migrate in restartable batches, then remove old columns or constraints only after old app versions are gone. A down migration is not automatically safe after data has been transformed or discarded.', '', 'System-versioned temporal tables store row versions using system period timestamps; they help answer “what did the row look like at time T?” They do not identify which human caused the change, do not replace audit controls, and can lose old versions under retention. Temporal time is based on transaction begin time, so it is not a commit-order ledger. Ledger, temporal history, CDC, and backup each preserve different evidence. `DBCC CHECKDB` checks consistency; only a tested restore demonstrates the recovery path. [Temporal tables](' + urls.temporal + ') [temporal queries](' + urls.temporalQuery + ')', '', '# Mechanisms and relationships', '', 'A useful incident decision tree is: if the goal is undoing bad data, select a recovery point from tested backups or a narrower history feature; if the goal is rapid service resumption, assess eligible failover and data-loss tradeoffs; if the goal is feeding another system, select a change movement contract; if the goal is safe deployment, stage schema/data compatibility. These paths can complement one another but are not interchangeable.', '', '# Worked examples', '', 'A team wants a 15-minute RPO and 30-minute RTO. It measures that log backups every five minutes meet its expected data-loss window, but a full database restore takes 90 minutes. It adds a tested DR replica to improve recovery time while protecting independent backups and rehearsing data-loss scenarios. It monitors send/harden/redo queues, runs a timed failover with client reconnection, and regularly restores a backup to isolated infrastructure. The replica improves availability; the isolated restore proves a different failure path.', '', '# Misconceptions and edge cases', '', '- “BACKUP succeeded” does not prove media, key, or restore sequence works.', '- `RESTORE VERIFYONLY` is not a substitute for restoring and running consistency/application checks.', '- A copy-only full backup does not become the differential base.', '- `WITH RECOVERY` ends a restore sequence; using it too early blocks applying later backups.', '- Synchronous mode alone does not guarantee automatic failover: synchronization, mode, policy, and quorum matter.', '- A replica can replicate an accidental delete or malicious update.', '- CDC and Change Tracking expose different information and neither is a whole-database backup.', '- Temporal history records database row versions, not actor identity, intent, or necessarily commit chronology.', '- `DBCC CHECKDB` is integrity evidence, not proof the backup can be restored.', '- Online index operations have version/edition/operation restrictions.', '', '# Knowledge checks with answers', '', '1. What must be true for a point-in-time restore under FULL? **A usable earlier data backup and an unbroken required log sequence covering the target.**', '2. Does a copy-only full reset the differential base? **No.**', '3. When do you use `WITH RECOVERY`? **After all intended backups are applied at the target recovery point.**', '4. Can a synchronous secondary automatically fail over while unsynchronized? **No; automatic failover requires an eligible synchronized target and required cluster conditions.**', '5. CDC versus Change Tracking? **CDC provides row-level change records; Change Tracking provides changed-key/version information for fetching current state.**', '6. What test proves a restore path? **An actual isolated restore followed by consistency/application validation and timing.**', '', '# Sources', '', bibliography].join('\n'));

for (const [groupId, items] of Object.entries(groups)) {
  const group = manifest.inventory.find(g => g.id === groupId);
  const comparison = groupId === 'backup-restore-and-recovery' ? 'A full backup is a restore anchor; a differential shortens data-restore work relative to its base; log backups provide continuity and point-in-time choices under FULL. Copy-only affects backup-chain bookkeeping, while VERIFYONLY checks media readability without performing recovery. These are complementary choices, not substitutes.' : groupId === 'availability-and-replication' ? 'Availability groups and FCIs are HA topologies with different instance/database and storage failover boundaries. Log shipping transfers log backups periodically. Replication, CDC, Change Tracking, and change event streaming are data movement/change-consumption approaches; their payload, latency, support status, and recovery guarantees differ. No row in this group makes a backup unnecessary.' : 'Schema migration controls deployment compatibility; temporal tables preserve prior row versions; database snapshots provide a dependent read-only point-in-time view; DBCC CHECKDB checks consistency. Each answers a different operational question.';
  const example = groupId === 'backup-restore-and-recovery' ? [
    "```sql",
    "-- Example pattern; replace paths, names, and sequence with verified backup metadata.",
    "RESTORE DATABASE [AppRestore] FROM DISK = N'D:\\backups\\App_full.bak' WITH NORECOVERY, MOVE N'App' TO N'D:\\data\\AppRestore.mdf', MOVE N'App_log' TO N'D:\\data\\AppRestore_log.ldf';",
    "RESTORE DATABASE [AppRestore] FROM DISK = N'D:\\backups\\App_diff.bak' WITH NORECOVERY;",
    "-- Apply every required log backup in LSN order, using the same STOPAT target.",
    "RESTORE LOG [AppRestore] FROM DISK = N'D:\\backups\\App_log_001.trn' WITH STOPAT = '2026-11-16T09:55:00', NORECOVERY;",
    "RESTORE LOG [AppRestore] FROM DISK = N'D:\\backups\\App_log_002.trn' WITH STOPAT = '2026-11-16T09:55:00', RECOVERY;",
    "```"
  ].join('\n') : groupId === 'availability-and-replication' ? [
    '```text', 'Incident: primary host fails.',
    '1. Check replica role, synchronization/redo state, WSFC quorum, and failover eligibility.',
    '2. If a synchronized automatic-failover target and policy conditions hold, allow/execute that path.',
    '3. If only an asynchronous lagging target is available, quantify potential loss before forced failover.',
    '4. Reconnect through the listener, validate writes and application health, then restore redundancy.', '```'
  ].join('\n') : [
    '```sql', '-- Query a system-versioned table for its state at a UTC point in time.',
    "SELECT OrderId, Status FROM dbo.Orders FOR SYSTEM_TIME AS OF '2026-11-15T10:00:00Z' WHERE OrderId = 42;", '```'
  ].join('\n');
  await write('reference-' + groupId, [head + ' — Reference: ' + group.title, '', '# Scope and version', '', 'SQL Server 2025 (17.x) current scope. This catalog explains only the exact supplied inventory. SQL Server edition, recovery model, database state, topology, and feature support can restrict commands; verify the linked statement and target version before production operations.', '', '# How this group fits the mental model', '', groupId === 'backup-restore-and-recovery' ? 'This inventory is the recovery recipe layer: data backups establish a starting state, log continuity advances that state, recovery options determine when it becomes usable, and validation proves the selected path.' : groupId === 'availability-and-replication' ? 'This inventory addresses service continuity and change movement. First identify the desired outcome (failover, read scale, subscriber, event consumer), then choose a mechanism with suitable RPO, latency, consistency, and operations.' : 'This inventory manages safe evolution and historical/integrity evidence: deploy compatible schema steps, keep row-history semantics explicit, and rehearse recovery independently.', '', '# Supplied names explained', '', items.map(itemBullet).join('\n\n'), '', '# Cross-item comparison', '', comparison, '', '# Examples', '', example, '', groupId === 'backup-restore-and-recovery' ? 'The restore sample assumes the backup media and file logical names were verified first, an isolated target is authorized, the log chain is complete, and encryption keys are available. Never run an overwrite/replace operation based only on a copied example. If there are more log backups, apply all in sequence and use the same STOPAT target on each. The sample is a syntax pattern, not a claim of execution. [Restore sequence guidance](' + urls.pointRestore + ')' : groupId === 'availability-and-replication' ? 'Failover should be exercised with clients and dependencies, not just a role-change command. Read-only routing requires driver intent and routing configuration; results on a secondary may be stale. [Failover conditions](' + urls.failover + ')' : 'Temporal query output can contain multiple versions per key. Include an explicit time predicate and understand whether the business asks for a point snapshot or all versions within/overlapping an interval. [Temporal query semantics](' + urls.temporalQuery + ')', '', '# Common mistakes', '', '- Treating VERIFYONLY, a snapshot, a replica, or an audit history table as a complete restore test.', '- Losing the certificate/private key needed for encrypted backup restore.', '- Applying a log backup out of order, choosing the wrong differential base, or recovering too early.', '- Using INIT, FORMAT, REPLACE, or RECOVERY without checking their overwrite/finality consequences.', '- Assuming synchronous means every replica is synchronized or every failover is automatic.', '- Assuming read-intent routing guarantees freshness or a specific secondary.', '- Treating CDC and Change Tracking as equivalent or as independent backups.', '- Treating system time as actor identity or commit ordering.', '- Deploying a destructive schema change before old application versions are retired.', '', '# Knowledge checks with answers', '', groupId === 'backup-restore-and-recovery' ? '1. What makes a differential applicable? **Its designated base full backup and matching database lineage.**\n2. What is the final action after applying all restore backups? **Recover the database with `WITH RECOVERY`.**\n3. Does VERIFYONLY prove structural consistency? **No; restore and validate the database.**\n4. What if a bulk-logged operation is in a required log backup? **You cannot stop within that backup at a point in time; recover through its end.**' : groupId === 'availability-and-replication' ? '1. What does synchronous commit acknowledge? **The configured synchronous secondary has hardened log, when synchronization is healthy.**\n2. Can forced failover lose data? **Yes, transactions not hardened on the target can be lost.**\n3. Does Change Tracking store full before/after rows? **No; it reports changed keys/version information.**\n4. What is the main FCI/AG distinction? **FCI is instance failover, typically shared storage; AG replicates selected databases between instances.**' : '1. What does `AS OF` answer? **Which row version was valid at a particular time.**\n2. Does temporal history name the human actor? **No.**\n3. Is a database snapshot an independent backup? **No; it depends on source storage/database.**\n4. Why is CHECKDB not a recovery test? **It checks consistency, not whether backup media and keys restore under the RTO.**', '', '# Sources', '', bibliography].join('\n'));
}

await write('practice', [head + ' — Exercises and worked solutions', '', '# Goals', '', 'Choose and validate recovery paths, reason about failover data loss, and design compatible deployments.', '', '# Worked example', '', 'An application requires a 10-minute RPO and 45-minute RTO. Log backups currently run every 30 minutes and a full restore takes two hours. Do not declare compliance based on a replica screenshot. First test a full/differential/log restore and measure its exact duration, including keys, validation, and cutover. Adjust log frequency and retention to meet the data-loss objective; separately test a HA/DR path for the time objective. Restore backups to isolated infrastructure periodically, and run a failover test that measures synchronization lag and client reconnection. Keep both mechanisms because they cover different failures.', '', '# Exercises', '', 'Try each before reading the solution.', '', '1. A database uses FULL recovery. A transaction is accidentally committed at 10:04; logs are backed up every 5 minutes, and the latest full backup is from yesterday. What must be checked and restored to recover to 10:03?', '2. A new copy-only full backup completed. Which full backup should a differential backup normally use as its base?', '3. A synchronous secondary is not synchronized and the primary is lost. Can the team claim zero-data-loss automatic failover?', '4. The primary is stable, but a deletion replicated to all secondaries. Which mechanism is most directly useful for recovering the database to just before the deletion?', '5. Design an expand-and-contract migration to rename a column while old and new application versions overlap.', '6. A temporal table query using `BETWEEN` returns a row version that starts exactly at the upper time. Why can that differ from `FROM ... TO`?', '7. A restore test passes VERIFYONLY but actual restore fails because the certificate is missing. What process was missing?', '8. A production deployment performs one long backfill transaction. Name failure risks and a better pattern.', '', '# Progressive hints', '', '1. Verify backup chain, log sequence and target coverage, log contents, and any intervening differential; apply data and logs in order.', '2. Copy-only does not reset the regular differential base.', '3. Automatic failover has eligibility requirements; forced promotion is a different operation.', '4. Replicas may preserve the same bad change; recover independent backups to an isolated target.', '5. Add first, support both shapes, backfill resumably, switch reads/writes, retire old binaries, then contract.', '6. BETWEEN includes the upper endpoint in its temporal overlap predicate; FROM TO excludes the upper boundary.', '7. Key/certificate export, separate custody, access test, and actual restore rehearsal.', '8. Log growth, lock duration, blocking, rollback time, and restart difficulty; use idempotent batches/checkpoints.', '', '# Complete solutions', '', '1. Identify the most recent usable full and applicable differential, then every required log backup through the target time. Verify database identity, recovery fork/LSNs, the chain, target coverage, backup media and encryption key. Restore to an isolated target, applying data/logs with NORECOVERY and the consistent STOPAT target, then RECOVERY; validate before cutover. Yesterday’s full can be used if every required backup is available.', '2. The last conventional full backup, because the copy-only full does not become the differential base.', '3. No. Automatic no-data-loss failover requires a configured synchronous, synchronized eligible target and cluster conditions. An unsynchronized target may require forced failover, which can lose transactions.', '4. Restore independent backups to a point before the deletion, then validate and reconcile later legitimate writes. A temporal history table may help recover specific row values if enabled and retained, but it is not a complete database recovery path.', '5. Add the new column while retaining the old one; deploy code that reads/writes compatibly; backfill in idempotent batches with checkpoints; switch new code to the new column; verify all old app instances/workers are gone; then enforce constraints and drop the old column in a later deployment. Keep forward recovery and data-reconciliation plans.', '6. SQL Server temporal period predicates treat BETWEEN’s upper endpoint inclusively, while FROM ... TO excludes its upper endpoint. Use the clause whose interval boundary semantics match the requirement and test exact boundary times. [Temporal query rules](' + urls.temporalQuery + ')', '7. Key lifecycle and actual recovery validation: store and protect the required certificate/private key separately, ensure restore operators can access it, and restore to isolated infrastructure. VERIFYONLY does not prove a database can be recovered.', '8. A long transaction can grow the log, hold locks, block other work, take a long time to roll back, and lose all progress on restart. Use bounded idempotent batches, checkpoint progress, pace against workload headroom, and verify row counts/invariants per batch.', '', '# Tests and edge cases', '', '- Restore the newest and an older retained recovery point, including encrypted media.', '- Test a log backup with minimally logged operations under BULK_LOGGED before claiming PITR behavior.', '- Remove access to the primary environment during a DR drill to prove backup separation.', '- Test failover with loss of quorum, lagging async replica, listener DNS/network, connection retry, and app write validation.', '- Run old and new application versions concurrently during schema migration tests.', '- Test temporal predicates at exact period boundaries and confirm retention behavior.', '- Run CHECKDB and application-level consistency checks on restored copies.', '', '# Reflection questions', '', 'What is the data-loss limit? What exact components contribute to restoration time? Which failure can affect both primary and replicas? Which key is needed to read the backup? Can old and new code coexist for every deployment step? What evidence proves the recovery path?', '', '# Sources', '', bibliography].join('\n'));

await write('interview', [head + ' — Interview questions and reasoning', '', '# Core questions', '', '1. Why is a full backup alone insufficient for point-in-time recovery under FULL?', '2. Differential versus log backup?', '3. What does VERIFYONLY prove?', '4. Why are backups and replicas both needed?', '5. Synchronous versus asynchronous availability-group commit?', '6. What conditions are required for automatic failover?', '7. CDC versus Change Tracking?', '8. How would you roll out a column rename without downtime?', '9. Temporal tables versus audit/ledger?', '10. What does DBCC CHECKDB repair risk mean?', '', '# Concise interview answers', '', '1. FULL requires an unbroken log backup chain to reach times after the data backup.', '2. Differential captures changed data extents since its base full; log captures transaction-log continuity since the previous log backup.', '3. It checks media readability/completeness, not database structure or successful recovery.', '4. Replicas reduce outage time but can copy logical corruption; independent backups support recovery to retained points.', '5. Synchronous waits for secondary hardening when synchronized, increasing commit latency; asynchronous lets primary commit without waiting and can lag.', '6. Configured automatic mode, synchronous-commit target, synchronized databases, cluster quorum/health, and policy conditions.', '7. CDC exposes captured row change records; Change Tracking reports changed keys/version for clients to retrieve current state.', '8. Expand, deploy dual-compatible code, backfill in resumable batches, switch code, wait for old versions, then contract.', '9. Temporal keeps system-time row versions; audit records selected actions/identity, and ledger adds tamper-evidence. They solve different evidence requirements.', '10. Repair can remove unrecoverable data; restore from a known-good backup is preferred when possible.', '', '# Deep follow-up answers', '', 'Discuss recovery model and log-chain consequences, LSN/base/fork validation, STOPAT behavior, key custody, and restore rehearsals. State the RPO/RTO numerically and include detection, decision, redo, validation, and client cutover in RTO. For AGs, distinguish role, synchronization, commit mode, failover mode, WSFC quorum, and application routing. A listener does not guarantee every driver reconnects. Change Tracking does not preserve old row images; temporal retention can delete history. `DBCC CHECKDB` does not establish backup recoverability.', '', '# Scenario questions', '', '**Ransomware encrypts the primary and all attached storage.** Isolate credentials/media, restore from offline/immutable independent backups with required keys, check for clean restore points, validate integrity/application state, and rotate compromised secrets before reconnecting.', '', '**An async DR replica is several minutes behind when the primary is lost.** Report the measured lag and potential RPO breach, choose whether service restoration justifies forced failover, document possible missing transactions, and reconcile after recovery.', '', '**A deployment must change a large table while old instances remain active.** Use additive schema first, compatible application code, throttled idempotent backfill, observable checkpoints, then delay destructive cleanup until all old consumers are retired.', '', '# Tradeoffs and alternatives', '', 'Frequent log backups reduce data-loss windows but increase management and storage requirements. Synchronous replicas reduce loss when synchronized but add latency and distance sensitivity. Async replicas increase distance tolerance at nonzero RPO risk. CDC offers row change history; Change Tracking offers a lighter sync signal. Temporal tables support row reconstruction but require retention and do not establish actor identity. Restore tests and failover tests exercise different recovery paths.', '', '# Common weak answers', '', '- “We have backups because the job is green.”', '- “The replica is synchronous, so failover is always lossless.”', '- “VERIFYONLY proves the restore works.”', '- “CDC is our backup.”', '- “Temporal tables show who changed the row.”', '- “We can just roll back the migration script.”', '- “CHECKDB repaired it, so we know disaster recovery works.”', '', '# Self-check rubric', '', 'Score 0–2: (0) names a feature only; (1) states its mechanism but misses its failure boundary; (2) quantifies RPO/RTO, names prerequisites and data-loss/retention limits, and proposes a measurable rehearsal or rollback.', '', '# Sources', '', bibliography].join('\n'));

console.log('Wrote t03-014 drafts for ' + all.length + ' exact inventory entries across 7 lessons.');
